## Solution

We evaluate
$$L = \lim_{n \to \infty} \left( \frac{(2n)!}{2^n \cdot n!} \right)^{\frac{1}{n}} \left( \tan\!\left( \frac{\pi\,\sqrt[n+1]{(n+1)!}}{4\,\sqrt[n]{n!}} \right) - 1 \right).$$

### Step 1. Asymptotics of the first factor

Let $c_n = \left(\dfrac{(2n)!}{2^n\, n!}\right)^{1/n}$. Using Stirling's formula $\ln(n!) = n\ln n - n + \tfrac12\ln(2\pi n) + O(1/n)$:

$$\ln c_n = \frac{1}{n}\bigl(\ln(2n)! - n\ln 2 - \ln n!\bigr).$$

Substituting,
$$\ln(2n)! = 2n\ln 2 + 2n\ln n - 2n + \tfrac12\ln(4\pi n) + O(1/n),$$
$$\ln n! = n\ln n - n + \tfrac12\ln(2\pi n) + O(1/n).$$

Hence
$$\ln c_n = \frac{1}{n}\Bigl(n\ln 2 + n\ln n - n + \tfrac12\ln 2 + O(1/n)\Bigr) = \ln 2 + \ln n - 1 + \frac{\ln 2}{2n} + O(1/n^2).$$

Therefore
$$c_n = \frac{2n}{e}\left(1 + \frac{\ln 2}{2n} + O(1/n^2)\right) = \frac{2n}{e}\bigl(1 + O(1/n)\bigr). \tag{1}$$

### Step 2. Asymptotics of the ratio inside the tangent

Let $b_n = (n!)^{1/n}$ and $a_n = b_{n+1}/b_n$. From Stirling,
$$b_n = \frac{n}{e}\exp\!\left(\frac{\ln(2\pi n)}{2n} + O(1/n^2)\right), \qquad b_{n+1} = \frac{n+1}{e}\exp\!\left(\frac{\ln(2\pi(n+1))}{2(n+1)} + O(1/n^2)\right).$$

The difference of the two logarithmic corrections is $O(\ln n / n^2) = o(1/n)$, so
$$a_n = \frac{n+1}{n}\bigl(1 + o(1/n)\bigr) = \left(1 + \frac{1}{n}\right)\bigl(1 + o(1/n)\bigr) = 1 + \frac{1}{n} + o(1/n). \tag{2}$$

### Step 3. Expansion of the tangent term

Set $\theta_n = \dfrac{\pi\, a_n}{4}$. From (2),
$$\theta_n = \frac{\pi}{4} + \frac{\pi}{4n} + o(1/n) = \frac{\pi}{4} + \varepsilon_n, \qquad \varepsilon_n = \frac{\pi}{4n} + o(1/n).$$

Since $\tan'(\pi/4) = \sec^2(\pi/4) = 2$, a first-order expansion gives
$$\tan\!\left(\frac{\pi}{4} + \varepsilon_n\right) = 1 + 2\,\varepsilon_n + O(\varepsilon_n^2),$$
so
$$\tan(\theta_n) - 1 = 2\,\varepsilon_n + O(\varepsilon_n^2) = \frac{\pi}{2n} + o(1/n). \tag{3}$$

### Step 4. The product

Combining (1) and (3):
$$c_n\bigl(\tan(\theta_n) - 1\bigr) = \frac{2n}{e}\bigl(1 + O(1/n)\bigr)\cdot\left(\frac{\pi}{2n} + o(1/n)\right) = \frac{\pi}{e}\bigl(1 + o(1)\bigr) \longrightarrow \frac{\pi}{e}.$$

The $O(1/n)$ correction in $c_n$ contributes $O(1/n)\to 0$, and the $o(1/n)$ correction in the tangent term contributes $o(1)\to 0$ after multiplication by $2n/e$.

### Conclusion

$$\boxed{\dfrac{\pi}{e}}$$

